To make requests with custom headers, you create a client and use `http.NewRequest` to create a request object, then add headers using `req.Header.Add()`, and finally execute it with `client.Do(req)`.\n\n## Basic Example\n\nThe `Client.Get()` method internally uses `NewRequest()` to create a GET request and then calls `c.Do(req)` to execute it.\n\nHere's the recommended pattern:\n\n```go\nclient := &http.Client{}\nreq, err := http.NewRequest(\"GET\", \"http://example.com\", nil)\nif err != nil {\n    // handle error\n}\n\n// Add custom headers\nreq.Header.Add(\"Accept\", \"application/json\")\nreq.Header.Add(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer token\")\n\n// Execute the request\nresp, err := client.Do(req)\nif err != nil {\n    // handle error\n}\ndefer resp.Body.Close()\n```\n\n## Key Points\n\n1. To make a request with custom headers, use NewRequest and Client.Do\n2. You access the headers using `req.Header`, and can set values using `req.Header.Set()` or `req.Header.Add()`\n3. You can create a wrapper/middleware for the http.Client with custom methods that add headers in a centralized location\n\nThis pattern gives you full control over all aspects of your HTTP request, including custom headers, timeouts, and other client configuration.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2f9e3e06-b069-46b1-9c06-c5213c5a7c4e","timestamp":"2026-05-11T10:34:34.914Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"go-github NewRequest Do method client.Get pattern headers","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_016MUetbLjcYx2j86Yf9aXir","content":[{"title":"http package - net/http - Go Packages","url":"https://pkg.go.dev/net/http"},{"title":"Simple Go HTTP Requests “GET” and Response Handling | by Naufal Al Hakim | Medium","url":"https://medium.com/@hakimnaufal/simple-go-http-requests-get-and-response-handling-b6fd83d62a5e"},{"title":"go/src/net/http/client.go at master · golang/go","url":"https://github.com/golang/go/blob/master/src/n